From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 19:10:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Replace the pandas APIs pandas 3.0 changed or deleted

Upstream capped `pandas[hdf5,performance]>=2.2,<3` and "Loosen dependency
bounds" removed the cap on the grounds that nothing in the code depends on
the capped major being absent. That was asserted rather than checked, and it
is wrong for three call sites. The recipe solves pandas 3.0.5.

Two of the three raise, one is silent, and the silent one is the one that
matters most.

* `utils/skeleton.py`, `SkeletonBuilder.pick_labeled_frame` -- silent, then
  fatal two lines later. `DataFrame.stack()` swapped to the `future_stack`
  implementation in 3.0, which no longer drops rows that are entirely NA.
  The function masks the per-animal label counts down to the maximum with
  `.where` and relied on `stack()` dropping those NaNs, so only maximally
  labelled `(frame, animal)` pairs survived -- that is the whole of "Find the
  most 'complete' animal". On pandas 3 every pair survives, including animals
  with no labels at all, and the pick is uniform over all of them. `__init__`
  then reshapes the picked animal's coordinates and hands them to
  `scipy.spatial.KDTree`, which rejects NaN, so the loud failure is
  `ValueError: data must be finite, check for nan or inf values` in a
  function that never mentions pandas. On a synthetic four-animal project
  where exactly one `(frame, animal)` pair is complete, 20 draws out of 20
  pick an incomplete animal on 3.0.5 against 0 out of 20 on 2.2.3, and the
  constructor completes on 2.2.3 and raises on 3.0.5. `SkeletonBuilder` is
  exported from the top level and is the GUI's "Build skeleton" button.

  `mask.stack(future_stack=True).dropna()` restores the drop the old
  implementation performed. `future_stack=True` is accepted by 2.2 and by 3.0
  -- where it is the default -- and the `.dropna()` is a no-op under the old
  implementation, so the result is index-for-index the pandas 2.2 list on
  both versions. Spelling it explicitly also silences the deprecation
  FutureWarning a bare `stack()` raises on 2.2.

* `gui/tracklet_toolbox.py`, `TrackletVisualizer.export_to_training_data` --
  `DataFrame.groupby(axis=...)` was deprecated in 2.1 and deleted in 3.0:
  `TypeError: DataFrame.groupby() got an unexpected keyword argument 'axis'`.
  Reached from the "Refine tracklets" GUI, `gui/tabs/refine_tracklets.py`.
  The replacement is `df.T.groupby(...).apply(...).T`, the idiom the rest of
  the package already uses (`refine_training_dataset/outlier_frames.py`,
  `utils/skeleton.py`, `utils/make_labeled_video.py`), with the inner
  function's `cols.iloc[:, 2]` and `cols.loc[mask]` transposed to
  `rows.iloc[2]` and `rows.loc[:, mask]`. Checked rather than assumed: on
  2.2.3, with the individuals and bodyparts deliberately out of alphabetical
  order so a regrouping would show, the rewrite gives the same column order,
  the same values and the same NaN count as the original.

* `benchmark/metrics.py`, `_format_gt_data` -- `DataFrame.stack(dropna=...)`
  is likewise gone: `ValueError: dropna must be unspecified as the new
  implementation does not introduce rows of NA values`. The call needs the
  rectangular result to reshape to `(n_frames, n_animals, n_bodyparts, 2)`,
  which is exactly what `dropna=False` bought. `future_stack=True` gives a
  frame that `.equals()` the `dropna=False` one on 2.2.3 and is the default
  on 3.0.

What was checked and is fine, so that the tail is known rather than hoped:

* Diffing `dir()` of the top-level namespace, `DataFrame`, `Series`, `Index`,
  `MultiIndex`, `DataFrameGroupBy`, `HDFStore` and the option namespaces
  between 2.2.3 and 3.0.5 gives `applymap`, `append`, `bool`, `first`,
  `last`, `pad`, `backfill`, `swapaxes`, `to_gbq`/`read_gbq`, `Series.view`,
  `Series.ravel`, `Index.format`, `Index.sort`, `Index.is_*`,
  `holds_integer`, `GroupBy.grouper`, `GroupBy.dtypes`, `GroupBy.fillna`,
  `options.mode.use_inf_as_na`, `options.mode.data_manager` and top-level
  `value_counts`. None is called anywhere in the package; the `.append(`,
  `.swapaxes(`, `.sort(`, `.view(` and `.pad(` hits are all list, NumPy,
  PyTorch or TensorFlow.
* `np.std` inside `df.agg([...])` really does change meaning -- 2.2 routed it
  to `Series.std` (ddof=1) and 3.0 calls NumPy (ddof=0), a silent
  `sqrt(n/(n-1))` shift. The only occurrence is
  `pose_estimation_tensorflow/core/evaluate_multianimal._compute_stats`,
  which nothing calls at 3.0.1, so it is left alone rather than fixed
  blind.
* `pose_estimation_tensorflow/core/evaluate.py` picks up rows from
  `.stack(level="individuals")` that it used to drop, but its only consumer
  is `.mean()`, which skips NA; the per-keypoint error table is numerically
  identical on both versions.
* The three sites carrying upstream's own "this will break on pandas 3"
  TODOs -- the `future.no_silent_downcasting` option context and
  `.infer_objects(copy=False)` in `evaluate_multianimal.py`, and
  `select_dtypes(include="object")` in
  `inferenceutils.parse_ground_truth_data_file` -- all still behave
  identically on 3.0.5. `select_dtypes(include="object")` still matches
  columns that are now reported as the new `str` dtype, and `.replace` of a
  `str`-dtype column against an int mapping still yields int64.
* `.to_numpy()` and `.values` come back read-only under copy-on-write on 3.0.
  A sweep over the 45 pandas-importing modules for a mutation of such a
  result finds nothing the backported CoW guards missed; every hit is a
  variable rebound to `np.zeros` first.
* `to_hdf`/`read_hdf` and `to_csv`/`read_csv` round-trips of the keypoint
  frames are value-, index- and column-identical, including through a
  string-valued coordinate column.
* The upstream test suite at v3.0.1 gives an identical result on pandas 2.2.3
  and 3.0.5 -- same 13 failures, 3380 passes, 32 skips, 5 xfails and 25
  errors, by name -- before and after this patch. That is why these three
  sites had to be found by hand.

`tests/test_pandas3_api.py` covers the three, and pairs two kinds of check
because the two failure modes need different ones. Running a call site
catches a deleted keyword, but only on pandas >= 3, so each deleted keyword
also gets an AST check over the whole package that is red on any pandas
version; the silent `stack` change has no exception to catch, so those tests
assert which frame is picked. `tracklet_toolbox.py` needs a Qt binding to
import, so its statement is lifted out of the module's AST and executed
directly -- the shipped source runs, without Qt.

Reverting each hunk on its own, on pandas 3.0.5:

* `utils/skeleton.py` -- `test_pick_labeled_frame_only_ever_returns_a_maximally_labeled_animal`,
  `test_pick_labeled_frame_single_animal_branch` and
  `test_skeleton_builder_constructs_on_a_mostly_unlabeled_project` fail, the
  last with the `KDTree` `ValueError`.
* `gui/tracklet_toolbox.py` -- `test_export_to_training_data_blanks_uncertain_keypoints`
  fails with the `groupby` `TypeError`, and `test_no_call_passes_axis_to_groupby`
  fails on pandas 2.2.3 as well.
* `benchmark/metrics.py` -- `test_format_gt_data_keeps_a_row_for_every_animal`
  fails with the `stack` `ValueError`, and `test_no_call_passes_dropna_to_stack`
  fails on pandas 2.2.3 as well.

All seven pass on 2.2.3 and on 3.0.5, and the full suite is unchanged on both.
